%I A193995 #15 Sep 06 2026 00:52:41
%S A193995 1,9,13,8,12,17,6,13,12,2,10,13,3,14,11,8,13,11,5,8,13,14,4,9,11,10,
%T A193995 14,4,10,13,3,4,12,12,13,16,8,10,13,9,14,8,12,5,15,12,11,14,5,12,11,
%U A193995 11,13,15,13,10,12,8,10,17,9,10,16,12,10,15,10,3,13,6
%N A193995 a(n) = number of (distinct) terms required to determine whether n is a happy number or not (A007770 or A031177).
%C A193995 Original name: Maximum number of terms required to determine whether a number is happy or not (A007770 or A031177).
%C A193995 The number 1 is the only term requiring just one term to determine if it is happy. Although the b-file appears to show that there are only a few numbers having 8 terms, see A193996 for more beginning at 78999.
%C A193995 The "number of terms" in the definition includes the initial value and the iterates under A003132, until 1 or an earlier occurring value is reached, but the repeated value is not counted once more. - _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 03 2026
%H A193995 T. D. Noe, <a href="/A193995/b193995.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A193995 a(n) = A099645(n) + A031176(n) for n >= 1. - _Jason (Haoxuan) Dong_, Sep 03 2026
%e A193995 For the initial value n = 2, the values obtained by repeatedly taking the sum of squares of digits (A003132) are 2, 4, 16, 37, 58, 89, 145, 42, 20, 4, ... from where on they repeat, so there are a(2) = #{2, 4, 16, 37, 58, 89, 145, 42, 20} = 9 distinct values.
%t A193995 f[n_] := Total[IntegerDigits[n]^2]; Table[Length[NestWhileList[f, n, UnsameQ, All]] - 1, {n, 100}]
%o A193995 (PARI) apply( {A193995(n, S=[n])=for(i=1,oo,setsearch(S,n=A003132(n)) && return(i); S=setunion(S, [n]))}, [1..90]) \\ with A003132=n->vecsum([d^2|d<-digits(n)]). - _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 03 2026
%Y A193995 Cf. A003132 (the iterated map: sum squares of digits), A364682 (number of iterations to reach a cycle).
%K A193995 nonn,base,changed
%O A193995 1,2
%A A193995 _T. D. Noe_, Aug 23 2011
